            <ArticleTitle>[Course of operative treatment of a sacral insufficiency fracture : Successful or serious treatment?]</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>890-895</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00113-017-0403-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A monolateral sacrum fracture was primarily diagnosed with a CT and treated with PMMA augmented SI screw fixation. The following CT showed an unexpected contralateral fracture which led to the need for a lumbopelvic stabilization. In the course of 6 months, successively occurring adjacent fractures required recurrent vertebroplasty. Most of these fractures could only be diagnosed through MRI. It remains unclear, whether initially even the contralateral sacral ala was fractured.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
